--TEST--
`new` takes the same visibility test a call does, so a `private` constructor is the singleton it was written to be — `rule:core-api/written-visibility`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// Nothing names `constructor` at the `new` site, but the constructor is what
// `new` resolves to — so skipping the test for want of a written member name
// would make `private` there mean nothing at all.
class Config {
    private function constructor() {}
    public static function load(): Config { return new Config(); }
}
Config $c = new Config();
echo "never reached", "\n";
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0471]: `Config::constructor()` is `private`, so only `Config`'s own bodies reach it
%A
